# {{n-g|To [[position#Verb|position]] or be positioned physically:}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To [[support]] oneself on the [[foot|feet]] in an [[erect]] position.
##: {{ux|en|Here I '''stand''', wondering what to do next.}}
##: {{ux|en|I can't reach the celing. Get me a chair to '''stand''' on.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Then came a maid with hand-bag and shawls, and after her a tall young lady. She '''stood''' for a moment holding her skirt above the grimy steps,{{nb...}}, and the light of the reflector fell full upon her.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=February|author=R. K. Evans|title=The role of research on British Railways|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=93|text=At one time a "standard test" for carriage riding was to stand a pencil on end on the compartment floor, or to measure how long it was possible to '''stand''' on one leg without touching the corridor walls; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Doug Criss|title=Here’s why students don’t have to recite the Pledge of Allegiance|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2019/02/19/us/pledge-of-allegiance-explainer-trnd/index.html|date=2019-02-19|passage=It’s recited in schools across the US every day by students '''standing''' stiffly with their hands over their hearts.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To rise to one’s feet; to [[stand up]].
##: {{ux|en|'''Stand''' up, walk to the refrigerator, and get your own snack.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To [[remain]] motionless.
##: {{ux|en|Do not leave your car '''standing''' in the road.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Matthew|2|9|text=The star, which they saw in the east, went before them, till it came and '''stood''' over where the young child was.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=23|passage=The slightest effort made the patient cough. He would '''stand''' leaning on a stick and holding a hand to his side, and when the paroxysm had passed it left him shaking.}}
##* {{RQ:Vance Outsider|page=382|column=1|passage=Turning back, then, toward the basement staircase, she began to grope her way through blinding darkness, but had taken only a few uncertain steps when, of a sudden, she stopped short and for a little '''stood''' like a stricken thing, quite motionless save that she quaked to her very marrow in the grasp of a great and enervating fear.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be placed in an [[upright]] or [[vertical]] orientation.
##* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=They burned the old gun that used to '''stand''' in the dark corner up in the garret, close to the stuffed fox that always grinned so fiercely. Perhaps the reason why he seemed in such a ghastly rage was that he did not come by his death fairly. Otherwise his pelt would not have been so perfect.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|1|passage=He seized the gun which always '''stood''' in a corner of his bedroom {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[place]] in an upright or standing position.
##: {{ux|en|He '''stood''' the broom in a corner and took a break.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=February|author=R. K. Evans|title=The role of research on British Railways|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=93|text=At one time a "standard test" for carriage riding was to '''stand''' a pencil on end on the compartment floor, or to measure how long it was possible to stand on one leg without touching the corridor walls; [...].}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[occupy]] or [[hold]] a [[place]]; to be [[set]], [[place]]d, [[fix]]ed, [[locate]]d, or [[situate]]d.
##: {{ux|en|Paris '''stands''' on the Seine.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1774|author=w:Edward Long|title=The History of Jamaica. Or, General Survey of the Antient and Modern State of that Island|section=volume 2, book 2, chapter 7, {{gbooks|xr0NAAAAQAAJ|6|stands}}|passage=The chapel '''ſtands''' on the South ſide of the ſquare, near the governor’s houſe.}}
##* '''2017''' October 2, "[http://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-41466116 Las Vegas shooting: At least 58 dead at Mandalay Bay Hotel]", in {{monospace|bbc.com}}, {{w|BBC}}:
##*: Las Vegas police say the number of people injured now '''stands''' at 515.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[measure]] when erect on the feet.
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Maud|part=XIII|stanza=1|page=44|passage=His face, as I grant, in spite of spite / Has a broad-blown comeliness, red and white, / And six feet two, as I think, he '''stands'''; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|of tears, sweat, etc.}} To be [[present]], to have [[well up|welled up]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=V|scene=vi|text=many an orphan’s water-'''standing''' eye}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1651|author=w:Francis Bacon|title=A True and Historical Relation of the Poysoning of Sir Thomas Overbury|location=London|publisher=John Benson & John Playford|chapter=Sir ''Jervas'' his Confession|page=71|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A28503.0001.001|passage=now my heart beginneth to melt within me being wounded (with that the tears '''stood''' in his eyes) to see the faces of some here present, whom J most earnestly love, and now must depart from with shame {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe Moll Flanders|page=222|passage=[He] pull’d me up again, and then giving me tvvo or three Kiſſes again, thank’d me for my kind yielding to him; and vvas ſo overcome with the Satisfaction and Joy of it, that I savv Tears '''ſtand''' in his Eyes.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Martin Chuzzlewit|32|page=380|text=He takes me half-price to the play, to an extent which I sometimes fear is beyond his means; and I see the tears a '''standing''' in his eyes during the whole performance {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Buchan Watcher|page=258|passage=How he escaped a broken neck in that dreadful place no human being will ever ken. The sweat, he has told me, '''stood''' in cold drops upon his forehead {{...}}}}
# {{n-g|To [[position#Verb|position]] or be positioned mentally:}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|followed by ''to'' + infinitive}} To be positioned to gain or lose.
##: {{ux|en|He '''stands''' to get a good price for the house.}}
## {{senseid|en|tolerate}} {{lb|en|transitive|negative polarity}} To [[tolerate]].
##: {{ux|en|I can’t '''stand''' when people don’t read the instructions.}}
##: {{ux|en|I can’t '''stand''' him.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=VII|passage=[I]f you call my duds a ‘livery’ again there'll be trouble. It's bad enough to go around togged out like a life saver on a drill day, but I can '''stand''' that 'cause I'm paid for it. What I won't '''stand''' is to have them togs called a livery.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To maintain one's ground; to be acquitted; not to fail or yield; to be safe.
##* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Addison|issue=291|date=2 February 1712|passage=[R]eaders by whose judgment I would '''stand''' or fall {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To maintain an invincible or permanent attitude; to be fixed, steady, or firm; to take a position in resistance or opposition.
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Esther|8|11|passage=The king granted the Jews{{...}}to gather themselves together, and to '''stand''' for their life.}}
##* {{RQ:South Sermons|sermonname=[Sermon preached at St. Mary’s Church in Oxon]|date=29 July 1660|passage=the '''standing''' pattern of their imitation}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative|obsolete}} To be in some particular state; to have essence or being; to be; to consist.
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Hebrews|9|10|passage=sacrifices{{...}}which '''stood''' only in meats and drinks}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|passage=Accomplish what your signs foreshow; / I '''stand''' resigned, and am prepared to go.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Woodstock|passage=Thou seest how it '''stands''' with me{{nb...}}, and that I may not tarry.}}
# {{n-g|To [[position#Verb|position]] or be positioned socially:}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket}} To act as an [[umpire]].
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[undergo]]; [[withstand]]; [[hold up]].
##: {{ux|en|The works of Shakespeare have '''stood''' the test of time.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|Theodore and Honoria|passage=Love '''stood''' the siege.}}
##* {{RQ:Addison Cato|scene=i|passage=Bid him disband his legions,{{...}}/ And '''stand''' the judgment of a Roman senate.}}
##* {{RQ:Pope Arbuthnot|lines=324–325|page=16|passage=That not for Fame, but Virtue's better end, / He '''ſtood''' the furious Foe, the timid Friend, {{...}}}}
## {{senseid|en|be candidate}}{{lb|en|intransitive|British}} To be a [[candidate]] (in an election).
##: {{ux|en|He is '''standing''' for election to the local council.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1678|author=w:Izaak Walton|title=The Life of Robert Sanderson
|passage=He '''stood''' to be elected one of the proctors of the university.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To remain [[valid]].
##: {{ux|en|What I said yesterday still '''stands'''.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To oppose, usually as a team, in competition.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1957|author=w:Matt Christopher|chapter=7|chapterurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=_WYkg6VyRXIC&pg=PT22|title=Basketball Sparkplug|passage="Kim, Jack, and I will '''stand''' you guys," Jimmie Burdette said. / "We'll smear you!" laughed Ron.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1973|author=R. J. Childerhose|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=Qqo_GB2hpPcC&q=%22will+stand+you%22|title=Hockey Fever in Goganne Falls|page=95|passage=The game stopped while sides were sorted out. Andy did the sorting. "Okay," he said. "Jimmy is coming out. He and Gaston and Ike and me will '''stand''' you guys."}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Louis Sachar|chapter=21|title=Sideways Stories from Wayside School|year=1978|page=86|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=Sd8CBkKmQ1UC&pg=PA86|passage="Hey, Louis," Dameon shouted. "Do you want to play kickball?" ¶ ""All right," said Louis. "Ron and I will both play." {{...}} "Ron and I will '''stand''' everybody!" Louis announced.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cover the expense of; to pay for.
##: {{ux|en|to '''stand''' a round of drinks}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''stand''' a treat}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''stand''' bail (security in respect of an arrested person)}}
##* {{RQ:Thackeray Newcomes|passage=I will either '''stand''' a glass of grog, or thou shalt pay the like for me, my lad}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have or maintain a position, order, or rank; to be in a particular relation.
##: {{ux|en|Christian charity, or love, '''stands''' first in the rank of gifts.}}
##* {{RQ:Atlantic|author=Connor Simpson|title=[[w:Barack Obama|Obama]] is neutral on Egypt right now|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804174156/https://www.theatlantic.com/international/archive/2012/09/obama-neutral-egypt-right-now/323687/|date=13 September 2022|passage=President Obama gave his first extended television interview since the protests in Libya and Egypt to Telemundo on Wednesday night, and he took the opportunity to explain the U.S.'s role in the developing world while clarifying where the country '''stands with''' Egypt right now.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be consistent; to agree; to accord.
##* {{RQ:Massinger Field Fatall Dowry|sig=I2|verso=1|page=69|passage=Doubt me not, by Heauen, I vvill doe nothing / But vvhat may '''ſtand''' vvith honour: {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To appear in [[court]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical}} Of a ship or its captain, to [[steer]], [[sail]] (in a specified direction, for a specified destination etc.).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1630|author=John Smith|title=True Travels|publisher=Kupperman|year_published=1988|page=40|passage=To repaire his defects, hee '''stood''' for the coast of Calabria, but hearing there was six or seven Galleyes at Mesina hee departed thence for Malta{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To remain without ruin or injury.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Cleomenes|passage=My mind on its own centre '''stands''' unmov'd.}}
#* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|canto=III|stanza=XXXII|passage=The ruin'd wall / Stands when its wind-worn battlements are gone.}}
# {{lb|en|card games}} To stop asking for more cards; to keep one's [[hand]] as it has been dealt so far.

=====Usage notes=====
* In older works, {{m|en|standen}} is found as a past participle of this verb; it is now archaic. The forms {{m|en|stooden}} and ''stand'' may also be found in dialectal speech; these are nonstandard.
* In {{senseno|en|tolerate}} it is a [[catenative verb]] that takes the gerund {{m|en||-ing}} or infinitive {{m|en||to...}}. See [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]].

