{{en-noun}}

# The [[act]] of [[standing]].
#* '''October 2, 1712''', {{w|Joseph Addison}}, ''The Spectator'' No. 499
#*: I took my '''stand''' upon an eminence{{...}}to look into their several ladings.
# A [[defensive]] position or [[effort]].
#: {{ux|en|The Commander says we will make our '''stand''' here.}}
# A [[resolute]], [[unwavering]] position; [[firm]] [[opinion]]; action for a purpose in the face of [[opposition]].
#: {{ux|en|They took a firm '''stand''' against copyright infringement.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=01 Dec 91|volume=19|number=20|journal=Gay Community News|page=9|title=A Great Need|author=Rebecca Levine;Judy Greenspan|text=There are also a growing number of lesbians in prison who are out about being lesbian and that '''stand''' in and of itself is much stronger than being out on the outside. These women are in much greater danger.}}
# A period of performance in a given location or [[venue]].
#: {{ux|en|They have a four-game '''stand''' at home against the Yankees.&emsp; {{nowrap|They spent the summer touring giving 4 one-night '''stands''' a week.}}}}
# {{senseid|en|device to hold something upright or aloft}} A device to hold something upright or [[aloft]].
#: {{ux|en|He set the music upon the '''stand''' and began to play.&emsp; {{nowrap|an umbrella '''stand''';}}&emsp; {{nowrap|a hat-'''stand'''}}}}
#* {{RQ:Belloc Lowndes Lodger|II|0091
|passage=There was a neat hat-and-umbrella '''stand''', and the stranger's weary feet fell soft on a good, serviceable dark-red drugget, which matched in colour the flock-paper on the walls.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=B.O.B Wild|title=The Dogs of War|page=69|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Dogs_of_War/wQQHAgAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=stand+in+the+shop&pg=PA69&printsec=frontcover|passage=Whilst I nipped out to get the booze Eddie would ease the large bread '''stand''' at the far end of the shop out from the counter so as to leave a gap behind, big enouh to take the basket. I would streak into the shop like a whippet and be behind the bread '''stand''' before you could blink.}}
# The platform on which a witness [[testify|testifies]] in [[court]]; the [[witness stand]] or [[witness box]].
#: {{ux|en|She took the '''stand''' and quietly answered questions.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Victoria Bekiempis|title=Bankman-Fried’s ex-girlfriend to take stand again after day of dramatic testimony|work=The Guardian|date=2023-10-11|url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/oct/11/caroline-ellison-testimony-sam-bankman-fried-trial|issn=0261-3077|passage=Shortly after Ellison started at Alameda around fall 2018 as a trader, she learned that the company was financially far sicker than she had known, she said on the '''stand'''.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} An area of raised seating for [[waiter]]s at the [[stock exchange]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|author=Julius E. Day|title=The Stockbroker's Office: Organisation, Management and Accounts|page=99
|passage=When a member has failed to comply with his bargains the fact is announced from one of the '''stands''', {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1934|author=Frances Cosgrove|title=Scenes for Student Actors: Dramatic Selections from New Plays|page=8
|passage=Just as that clock is striking now, the two waiters appear on the '''stands''' and take off their hats, as if to a corpse.}}
# A particular [[grove]] or other group of [[tree]]s or shrubs.
#: {{ux|en|This '''stand''' of pines is older than the one next to it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=James Beament|title=The Violin Explained: Components, Mechanism, and Sound|page=159
|passage=Pernambuco is a coastal wood which grows in maintained '''stands''' in Brazil.}}
# {{lb|en|forestry}} A contiguous group of trees sufficiently uniform in age-class distribution, composition, and structure, and growing on a site of sufficiently uniform quality, to be a distinguishable unit.
# A [[standstill]], a motionless state, as of someone confused, or a hunting dog who has found game.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1625|author=w:Francis Bacon|chapter=Of Truth|title=Essays
|passage=One of the later school of the Grecians, examineth the matter, and is at a '''stand''', to think what should be in it, that men should love lies; where neither they make for pleasure, as with poets, nor for advantage, as with the merchant; but for the lie’s sake.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1819|author=w:Lord Byron|title={{w|Don Juan (Byron)|Don Juan}}|section=I.168
|passage=Antonia's patience now was at a '''stand'''—<br> "Come, come, 't is no time now for fooling there,"<br> She whispered{{...}}}}
# A small building, [[booth]], or [[stage]], as in a [[bandstand]] or [[hamburger]] stand.
# A designated spot where someone or something may stand or wait.
#: {{ux|en|a [[taxi stand]]}}
# {{lb|en|US|dated}} The situation of a shop, store, hotel, etc.
#: {{ux|en|a good, bad, or convenient '''stand''' for business}}
# {{lb|en|US|historical}} {{short for|en|tavern stand||a roadside inn}}
# {{senseid|en|grandstand}}{{lb|en|sports}} [[grandstand|Grandstand]]. {{qualifier|often in the plural}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 11, 2011| author=Rory Houston| work=RTE Sport| title=[http://www.rte.ie/sport/soccer/2011/1111/estonia_ireland_report.html Estonia 0-4 Republic of Ireland]| passage=The end of the opening period was relatively quite [sic] as Vassiljev's desperate shot from well outside the penalty area flew into the '''stand''' housing the Irish supporters and then Ward's ctoss [sic] was gathered by goalkeeper Pareiko.}}
